        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "598fc6a4-c730-5310-8482-08beeacb5605",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Hawaiian tropical dry forests, leeward Hawaiian island slopes\n- Plants: Acacia koa, Acacia koaia, Euphorbia species, wiliwili\n- Products: Alaia boards\n- Concepts: Fabaceae\n\nFacts:\n- Hawaiian tropical dry forests have dominant tree species that include koa (Acacia koa), koaiʻa (Acacia koaia), and ʻakoko (Euphorbia species).\n- Acacia koa is a species of flowering tree in the pea family Fabaceae.\n- Wiliwili is typically found in Hawaiian tropical dry forests on leeward island slopes up to 600 meters in elevation.\n- Alaia boards were generally made from the wood of Acacia koa.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f7c1525a-f30e-5b30-84df-f1c6ce9f3ca0",
        "text": "Page: Hawaiian tropical dry forests. Sentence: Dominant tree species include koa (\"Acacia koa\"), koaiʻ a (\"A. koaia\"), ʻ akoko (\"Euphorbia\" spp.)\nPage: Acacia koa. Sentence: Acacia koa is a species of flowering tree in the pea family, Fabaceae.\nPage: Wiliwili. Sentence: It is typically found in Hawaiian tropical dry forests on leeward island slopes up to an elevation of 600 m .\nPage: Alaia. Sentence: The boards were between 7 and long, weighed up to 100 lb , and were generally made from the wood of Acacia koa.",
